<GuideLayout title="How to Measure a Shelf for Storage Bins" description="Measure usable inside width, height and depth so storage-bin layouts reflect the space you can really reach." canonicalPath="/measurement-guide/" eyebrow="Measurement guide">
  <div class="content-summary">
    <strong>Measure the usable opening, not the furniture exterior.</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>Use the narrowest clear width and height.</li>
      <li>Measure depth to the point where a door can still close.</li>
      <li>Use the widest external dimensions of every bin.</li>
      <li>Reserve clearance when a zero-gap fit would be difficult to handle.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>What ShelfSketch needs</h2>
  <p>The planner uses three inside measurements for the shelf or cabinet and three outside measurements for each object. Width and height determine the front-facing layout. Depth is checked separately as a hard limit. This prevents a box from appearing to fit simply because its front face is small enough.</p>

  <h2>Measure the clear inside width</h2>
  <p>Measure from the first obstruction on the left to the first obstruction on the right. In an open shelf that is normally the space between the side panels. In a cabinet, the opening can be narrower than the cavity because of door frames, hinges or a raised front lip.</p>
  <p>Take the measurement near the front, middle and back. Use the smallest value when the sides are not perfectly parallel. A box that fits at the front but jams halfway in is not a useful fit.</p>

  <h2>Measure the usable height</h2>
  <p>Measure from the surface the bin will stand on to the lowest obstruction above it. Include shelf supports, rails and any overhanging frame. If you plan to lift a lidded bin upward before pulling it forward, reserve enough vertical clearance for that motion.</p>

  <h2>Measure depth with the door in mind</h2>
  <p>Measure from the back wall or rear obstruction to the point where the front of the bin can sit without blocking a door, drawer front or sliding panel. A handle that projects forward counts as part of the depth. For a closed cabinet, test the door after placing a temporary object at the measured depth.</p>

  <h2>Measure bins at their widest external points</h2>
  <p>Retail listings sometimes emphasize internal capacity rather than the exact external footprint. For fit planning, measure the outside edges that can touch the furniture or another object. Check:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>handles and grip lips;</li>
    <li>lids and locking clips;</li>
    <li>feet, wheels and stacking rims;</li>
    <li>tapered sides that are wider at the top;</li>
    <li>soft bags that bulge when filled.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Choose practical clearance</h2>
  <p>A calculated exact fit may be physically possible but unpleasant to use. Horizontal clearance helps when boxes are not perfectly rectangular or when fingers need to reach between them. Vertical clearance helps with lifting and removing lids. Start with a modest margin and compare the Compact and Easy Access layouts.</p>

  <h2>Record measurements consistently</h2>
  <p>Use one unit system while measuring. ShelfSketch can display centimetres or inches, but it stores normalized measurements internally. Avoid rounding a product down. When a listing says 29.8 cm, enter 29.8 rather than 29.</p>

  <h2>A quick pre-purchase check</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Measure the furniture opening twice.</li>
    <li>Check the product’s external dimensions from more than one reliable source when possible.</li>
    <li>Add handles, lids or other projections that are not included clearly.</li>
    <li>Generate more than one layout.</li>
    <li>Verify the final combination physically before buying multiples.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="notice"><strong>Planning limitation</strong><br/>ShelfSketch models rectangular dimensions. Rounded corners, flexible sides, uneven shelves and real-world manufacturing tolerances can change the result.</div>
  <p><a class="button button-primary" href="/tool/">Open the fit calculator</a></p>
